extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Axes and gridlines"
	- pagedescription = "Styling the axes, the numbers and titles along them, and the gridlines in Tablechart."
	- const varieties = [['Elstar', '62', '1.20'], ['Jonagold', '75', '1.05'], ['Braeburn', '48', '1.45'], ['Gala', '55', '1.30'], ['Cox', '35', '1.85'], ['Discovery', '30', '1.75']]

mixin varieties
	table
		thead
			tr
				th Variety
				th Harvest per tree (kg)
				th Price per kg (€)
		tbody
			each row in varieties
				tr
					th= row[0]
					td= row[1]
					td= row[2]

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Axes and gridlines
				p.lead The axes, the numbers and titles along them, and the gridlines.

	+section("Showing axes and gridlines")
		p Axes and gridlines are not always shown by default. For all charts with a horizontal or vertical value axis, the main axis (the 'baseline') is always shown. You can see that in for example the column and line chart, it is at the bottom, in the bar chart it is at the left. But the value axis is not shown by default, unless the chart type really requires it, such as a #[a(href=url('/types/scatter.html')) scatter chart].
		
		p To turn the axes on, add #[code data-chart-axis]. To turn the grid on, add #[code data-chart-grid]. That works for every type except the donut.
		
		p To turn it on by default via the config, use the #[code axis] and #[code grid] options.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b Rainfall at the orchard
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '84'], ['Feb', '71'], ['Mar', '76'], ['Apr', '58'], ['May', '61'], ['Jun', '47'], ['Jul', '39'], ['Aug', '52']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]



	+section("The scale")
		p If a chart has an axis or gridlines, its scale ends on a number of the axis. For example, if the highest value is 84 and the numbers on the axis go up by 10, the scale ends at 90.
		p To set the scale yourself, add #[code data-chart-scale] to the figure, with the number where the scale starts and the number where it ends. Tablechart then chooses the numbers on the axis between them.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<figure data-chart="column" data-chart-axis data-chart-scale="0 100">

		p If you give three numbers or more, those are the numbers on the axis.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid data-chart-scale="0 25 50 75 100")
				figcaption: b Apples picked, out of 100
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If a value is higher than the end of your scale, the scale goes on to that value, so the bar or point is shown whole.
		p On a column, bar, line, area or waterfall chart, the scale always includes zero. On a scatter chart it does not. There #[code data-chart-scale] sets the axis up, and #[code data-chart-scale-x] the axis across.

	+section("The variables")
		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-color
					td The lines of the axes: the text colour at 55% by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-width
					td The width of the lines of the axes: #[code 1px] by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-size
					td The size of the numbers and titles along the axes: the size of the names by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-text-color
					td The colour of the numbers and titles along the axes: the text colour by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-grid-color
					td The colour of the gridlines: the text colour by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-grid-opacity
					td The opacity of the gridlines: #[code 0.09] by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-grid-width
					td The width of the gridlines: #[code 1px] by default

	+section("Softer axes, stronger gridlines")
		p In this example the axes are lighter and the gridlines are stronger.

		+style.
			.soft-axes {
				--tablechart-axis-color: color-mix(in oklab, currentColor 25%, transparent);
				--tablechart-grid-opacity: 0.25;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.soft-axes(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				+varieties

	+section("Dashed gridlines")
		p The gridlines are one #[code path] with the class #[code tablechart-grid]. To set more than the variables, such as dashes, use CSS on that class. A dash is in pixels, at any size of the chart.

		+style.
			.dashed-grid .tablechart-grid {
				stroke-dasharray: 1 3;
				opacity: 0.4;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.dashed-grid(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				+varieties

	+section("The numbers and titles")
		p #[code --tablechart-axis-size] sets the size of the numbers and the titles along the axes. To style them separately, use the class #[code tablechart-tick] for the numbers and #[code tablechart-axis-title] for the titles.

		+style.
			.small-axes {
				--tablechart-axis-size: 0.8em;
			}
			.small-axes .tablechart-axis-title {
				font-weight: 600;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.small-axes(data-chart="scatter")
				+varieties

		p If the numbers are smaller, there is room for more of them, so the axis may show more numbers.
		p To make the numbers lighter or darker than the names, set #[code --tablechart-axis-text-color]. The names have their own colour, #[code --tablechart-name-color].

		+style.
			.light-axes {
				--tablechart-axis-text-color: color-mix(in oklab, currentColor 55%, transparent);
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.light-axes(data-chart="column" data-chart-axis)
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Light and dark")
		p By default, the axes and gridlines use the text colour, so they change with the colour scheme. To set a colour for each scheme, use #[code light-dark()]. See #[a(href=url('/styling/light-dark.html')) Light and dark].

		+codeblock('CSS').
			:root {
				--tablechart-grid-color: light-dark(#16323f, #f3efe9);
				--tablechart-axis-color: light-dark(#8aa0aa, #5b6f78);
			}

	+section("Next")
		p See #[a(href=url('/reference/variables.html')) CSS variables] for all variables.
